* `gibbsf90()` now returns the pedigree of a genetic effect as `$pedigree`, as
`get_pedigree()` gives it for a `remlf90()` fit. When `build_pedigree()` had to
recode the pedigree, the levels of the genetic effect in `$solutions` are
breedR's internal codes, which can be other animals' ids, and nothing in the
result translated them; the id of level `k` is
`match(k, attr(res$pedigree, 'map'))` (#64)

* `gibbsf90(genomic = ...)` now derives the genomic XrefID from the model's
pedigree, as `remlf90()` does. It used to stop with "Cannot build the genomic
XrefID" unless the user supplied one (#55)

* The genomic QC report of `remlf90()` and `gibbsf90()`
(`fit$genomic$excluded_animals` and `fit$genomic$conflicts`) now names animals
by the ids given in the pedigree also when `build_pedigree()` had to recode it.
It used to show breedR's internal codes, which can be other animals' ids (#58)

* An AR `rho` grid search in `remlf90()`, which is also what an unset `rho`
runs, now works when `spatial` is a variable or is forwarded through `...` from
a wrapper function. Each candidate fit rewrote the `spatial = list(...)`
expression typed in the call, and otherwise failed with "object of type
'symbol' is not subsettable". Calls that type `spatial = list(...)` are
unchanged; for the others, the call stored in the result holds the evaluated
`spatial` list with the chosen `rho` (#5)

* `ranef()` now names the breeding values of a genetic effect, and their
standard errors, by the ids given in the pedigree also when `build_pedigree()`
had to recode it (gaps, codes not starting at 1, offspring coded below a
parent). They used to be named by breedR's internal codes, which can be other
animals' ids, so a lookup by id silently returned another animal's value. The
values and their order are unchanged; code that translated these names back
through `attr(get_pedigree(fit), 'map')` must drop that step (#63)

* `check_genetic()` no longer lets a data id of 0, a negative id or a
fractional id through when the pedigree has been recoded. The ids were used to
subscript the recode map, which drops a 0, treats a negative number as an
exclusion and truncates a fraction, so the check could pass on fewer ids than
records and the fit then failed with an unrelated error, or put a record on
another animal. Such ids now stop with "The following individuals in id are not
represented in the pedigree", naming them, as they already did when the
pedigree was not recoded (#62)

* `build_pedigree()` now stops with a message naming them when a parent code is
negative. Only 0 or NA mark an unknown parent; a negative parent code used to
fail with an unrelated error from pedigreemm or from indexing the recoding
map, and in a rare case the pedigree was returned with wrong parents (#66)

* `build_pedigree()` now stops with a message naming them when a code exceeds
`.Machine$integer.max`. Codes are stored as integers and the recoding map is
as long as the largest code, so such a pedigree needed a map of billions of
entries: it failed to allocate it, or took tens of GB (#67)

* `remlf90(cont = TRUE)` no longer stops with "infinite or missing values in
'x'" when resuming a multi-trait fit in which no record observes every trait,
such as site-as-trait data. The progress file supplies every initial
variance, so breedR's data-based default, which is undefined for such data,
is no longer computed and validated first. Fits with `cont = FALSE` are
unchanged (#71)

* `write_snp_file()` no longer writes some whole-number double animal ids
in scientific notation (100000 became "1e+05"), which made
`remlf90(genomic = ...)` stop with "Genotyped animals not found in the
pedigree" even though the pedigree was fine. Same R quirk as #43, in the
SNP writer rather than `build_pedigree()` (#54)

* `breedR.qget()` now reports the backend's own diagnostic when a submitted
fit failed during setup. BLUPF90+ can exit 0 on a fatal setup error (missing
data file, malformed parameter file); `parse_results()` used to crash on
"cannot open the connection" or "subscript out of bounds" instead of showing
the message that was in the log the whole time. The synchronous `remlf90()`
path already handled this (#22/#23) (#14)

* `breedR.sample.phenotype()` with `check.factorial = FALSE` and no spatial
effect no longer fails with "incorrect number of dimensions" when some founder
has no offspring; such founders are now dropped, as they already were with a
spatial effect (#61)

* `check_spatial()` now converts a fully-specified AR `rho` matrix or
data.frame to a data.frame before it is used as the candidate grid. Passed
through uncoerced, it reached `transform()` inside `remlf90()` only after
every candidate had already been fitted, and failed there with the
misleading error "object 'ans.rho' not found" (#4)

* `check_spatial()` now accepts a bare AR `rho` vector containing `NA`,
including `rho = c(NA, NA)`, documented as the exact equivalent of omitting
`rho`. It previously failed immediately with "length(rho) == 2 is not
TRUE" (#42)

* The `Additive Genetic Models in Mixed Populations` vignette builds the
mixed-population covariance of method 3 by splitting the relationship matrix by
founder population (`A = A_E + A_J`), instead of scaling its hybrid blocks by
averages of the two variances. Those factors matched the simulated covariance
only at `lambda = 1`, and left the offspring block non-positive-definite below
about 0.63. The fitted models, which use the pure offspring only, are
unchanged (#36)

* The shipped `inst/doc/Genomic-selection.R` script is now live: every
illustrative chunk (installing the genomic binaries, fitting ssGBLUP, running
the GWAS, prediction, Gibbs sampling) is runnable code instead of a comment,
and sourcing it no longer disables chunk evaluation for the rest of the
session. Both defects came from how the tangle was generated (a real
`rmarkdown::render()` let the vignette's build-time `eval = FALSE` default
leak into the tangled script) rather than from anything in the vignette's own
text (#30)

* `install_progsf90()`, `install_renumf90()` and `install_genomic_programs()`
now raise `options(timeout)` to at least 600s for the duration of each
binary download (never lowering a higher user setting), so the UGA
server's slow connection no longer truncates the larger binaries under
R's 60s default. README.md, `?install_progsf90` and `DESCRIPTION` now
document the Linux MKL/OpenMP runtime requirement and the current UGA
certificate-chain workaround (#69)

* `install_progsf90()` now defaults its download directory to the same option
`check_progsf90()` uses. With no `bin/` directory yet (e.g. after installing
with `BREEDR_SKIP_INSTALL_BINARIES=true`), the old default resolved to an
empty path, and `install_progsf90()` -- the command the error message itself
recommends -- failed with "zero-length path argument" (#7)

* `build.AR.rho.grid()` now honours the `ar.eval` option instead of a
hard-coded grid, so a custom grid set with `breedR.setOption("ar.eval", ...)`
is actually used. `breedR.setOption()` also stopped collapsing a numeric
vector value (such as `ar.eval`) down to its first element (#6)

* `neighbours.at()`'s list method rejected every list of matrices: its guard
compared `class()` against `'matrix'`, which has included `'array'` since
R 4.0 and so was never `TRUE`. Unreachable from within the package, which
only ever calls the matrix method; affected only direct callers and the
competition integration test's setup (#27)

* `remlf90()` accepts `traits`, a named list selecting the responses on which
each random-effect group is fitted, so an effect can be dropped from some
traits of a multi-trait model. Unlisted groups keep every trait; absent
coefficients and variances are reported as `NA` (#51)

* `gibbsf90()` now returns the per-round variance component samples for any
model. `res$samples` held round numbers and component counts instead of the
variances unless the model had exactly one random effect and a single residual
variance; a multi-trait model returned nothing at all (#77)

* `remlf90()`, `remlf90_from_renum()` and `validate_prediction()` now refuse
class-based heterogeneous residual variances (`hetres_options(group_col = ...)`,
`OPTION hetres_int`). BLUPF90+ reads them under REML but fits a homoscedastic
model, which used to be returned without comment; `gibbsf90()` estimates
them (#39)

* `ranef()` now names the standard errors of genetic and generic effects like
their values, and labels the rows of multi-trait genetic effects by animal.
Looking either up by name used to return another level's (#60)

* Fix a crash in `parse_results()` ("replacement has length zero") on a fit
whose AI matrix needed bending on its last round; the `-2logL` line is now
searched for instead of assumed at a fixed offset from the last round (#24)

* Fix a crash in the AR `rho` grid search ("attempt to select less than one
element in get1index") when every candidate rho produced no usable
log-likelihood; it now reports a clear error instead (#3)

* `predf90()`'s `dir` argument is now required, with no default. Each fit now
works in its own subdirectory of `tempdir()`, so there is no longer a
directory that a default could point to (#25)

* Parse large covariance matrices more robustly from REML logs (#101)

* Store breedR model and REML log in temp dir for recovery (#100)

* More informative error messages on specification of initial variances (#79)

* Bugfixes (#80, #85, #86)

* Improved documentation (Handling pedigrees)


breedR 0.12
===========

* Basic multitrait interface  (#30)

* Default values for initial (co)variances are now a function of empirical
(co)variances

* Interface for using **weights** for residual variance  (#77)

* Vignette: Heterogeneous variances


breedR 0.11
===========

* Install breedR from package repository avoiding package devtools

* New vignette: Computing Heritability

* Access to inverse Average-Information matrix

* Automatic computation of heritability for genetic models and method 'ai'

* remlf90() interface to PROGSF90 OPTIONS

* Much more exhaustive testing ensures greater stability.

* New datasets Douglas (multi-site) and Larix (repeated measurements)

* Separate R-package from BLUPF90 binaries. Exploit 64bit architectures.

* Several bugfixes. Notably, the empirical variogram computation and memory issues with the AR model.



breedR 0.10
===========

* Full internal refactoring (with few 'visible' changes in the interface)

* `model.matrix()` no longer separates `fixed` and `random` effects.
  It returns the same structure as `ranef()` which is more intuitive.

* `vcov()` works now with any of the non-diagonal random effects.


breedR 0.9
==========

* New 'generic' model component allows passing a list of arbitrary incidence and covariance/precision matrices

* Optimized splines model. It runs about 5x faster.


breedR 0.8
==========

* New interface ranef() to access BLUP of random effects

* New interface model.matrix() to get incidence matrices

* Integrated vignettes into the package


breedR 0.7
==========

* Remote computing through ssh

* Convenient scaling of spatial variance components

* Additive genetic competition model

* Permanent environmental competition effect

* Simulation infrastructure


breedR 0.6
==========

* Visualization and diagnostic tools

* Prediction in unobserved locations


breedR 0.5
==========

* Customizable initial variance components

* breedR.option infrastructure


breedR 0.4
==========

* AR1xAR1 spatial model

* Eucalyptus Globulus dataset and demo


breedR 0.3
==========

* Pedigree checking, completing, sorting and recoding


breedR 0.2
==========

* Unstructured Random effects

* Simple simulation of spatially structured effects

* Spatial module following Cappa and Cantet (2007) splines model


breedR 0.1
==========

* Basic wrapper to Mistalz's (AI)REMLF90

* Convenient 'metagene' class to represent and manipulate simulated datasets
